<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">German New Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1884 CE – 1914 CE · capital Finschhafen; later Rabaul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Germany's main Pacific colony, covering northeast New Guinea, the Bismarck Archipelago and much of Micronesia, until Australia and Japan seized it in 1914.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, named Kaiser-Wilhelmsland, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a>. The territory was run by the chartered New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> Company until 1899, when the Reich took over. That year Germany bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands">Northern Mariana Islands</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marshall-islands-protectorate">Marshall Islands Protectorate</a>, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-nauru">Nauru</a>, was merged in 1906.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Administration moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finschhafen">Finschhafen</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madang">Friedrich-Wilhelmshafen</a> (Madang), then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokopo">Herbertshöhe</a>, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a> in 1910. Copra plantations depended on recruited labour, often coerced, and phosphate was mined on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-nauru">Nauru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angaur">Angaur</a>. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-hahl">Albert Hahl</a>, from 1902 to 1914, extended control through appointed village headmen called luluai and a head tax. Lutheran and Catholic missions were active, and scientists studied the region's peoples.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In September 1914 an Australian force captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a> after fighting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bita-paka">Bita Paka</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> seized the Micronesian islands. After the war the lands became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-new-guinea">Territory of New Guinea</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-nauru">Territory of Nauru</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/german-new-guinea.jpg" alt="German New Guinea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Imperial Colonial Office of the German Empire Vector: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichskolonialflagge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1884 CE – 1914 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Finschhafen; later Rabaul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert Hahl</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous religions, Lutheranism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Tok Pisin, Papuan and Austronesian languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Papua New Guinea, Palau, Micronesia, Northern Mariana Islands, Marshall Islands, Nauru</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Deutsch-Neuguinea, Kaiser-Wilhelmsland</dd></dl></aside></div>
